<Component name="FreeTracing">
  <VStack padding="{responsive('0.75rem', '2rem')}" gap="1rem">

    <H1>
      Free tracing
    </H1>

    <Markdown>
      <![CDATA[
When you wrap a component with `wrapComponent` or `wrapCompound`, tracing comes for free. Every `didChange`, `gotFocus`, and `lostFocus` event emits a semantic trace. No extra code, no configuration. But what does that actually buy you?

## What a screenreader hears

The `aria-label` prop that SliderW forwards — and Slider silently drops — isn't cosmetic. It's the accessible name. When a blind user tabs to the SliderW below and adjusts it, their screenreader announces **"Volume, slider, 50"**. The hand-written Slider? It's an unlabeled control. The screenreader says **"slider, 50"** — no context, no way to distinguish it from any other slider on the page.

Open the inspector (cog icon) and interact with both sliders. SliderW's traces include the `aria-label` in the component context. Slider's traces — if they existed — wouldn't. But they don't exist at all, because hand-written renderers are invisible to the trace system.
      ]]>
    </Markdown>

    <Card var.sVal="" var.swVal="">
      <H2>
        Accessibility: labeled vs unlabeled
      </H2>
      <HStack wrapContent="{true}">
        <VStack width="{responsive('100%', '50%')}">
          <Text fontWeight="bold">
            Slider — screenreader says "slider, 50"
          </Text>
          <Slider
            aria-label="Volume"
            minValue="0"
            maxValue="100"
            initialValue="50"
            onDidChange="{(v) => sVal = v}" />
          <Text>
            Value: {sVal}
          </Text>
        </VStack>
        <SpaceFiller />
        <VStack width="{responsive('100%', '50%')}">
          <Text fontWeight="bold">
            SliderW — screenreader says "Volume, slider, 50"
          </Text>
          <SliderW
            aria-label="Volume"
            minValue="0"
            maxValue="100"
            initialValue="50"
            onDidChange="{(v) => swVal = v}" />
          <Text>
            Value: {swVal}
          </Text>
        </VStack>
      </HStack>
    </Card>

    <Markdown>
      <![CDATA[
## Before and after

Here's what the inspector shows for each slider. The hand-written **Slider** emits only engine internals — `handler:start`, `state:changes`, `handler:complete`. Useful for debugging the engine, but invisible to the semantic layer.

![Slider trace — engine internals only](slider-before.png)

**SliderW** adds `value:change didChange SliderW [Volume] "53"` — the semantic trace that names the component, includes the accessible name a screenreader would announce, and records the value. This is what trace-tools reads, what AIs reason about, and what makes regression testing possible.

![SliderW trace — semantic value:change event with accessible name](slider-after.png)

## Traces are catnip for AIs

Open the inspector and drag the SliderW thumb. You'll see `value:change` events with the component name, the old value, and the new value — a semantic log of everything the app is doing.

Export that trace and hand it to an AI. It doesn't need to read your source code or understand your component tree. The trace *is* the conversation: "the user moved Volume from 50 to 73, then tabbed to Brightness and set it to 40." Humans and AIs can talk about app behavior at this level without ambiguity.

Hand-written renderers emit nothing. The AI sees silence. With wrapped components, every interaction is narrated.

This matters for debugging — paste a trace into a chat and ask "why did the value jump from 30 to 100?" — but it matters even more for what comes next.

## Semantic regression testing

[trace-tools | target=_blank](https://github.com/xmlui-org/trace-tools) reads these traces and generates Playwright test scripts. The workflow:

1. A person who doesn't know Playwright performs a user journey in the app
2. The trace system captures every interaction semantically
3. trace-tools converts the trace into a Playwright script that replays the journey
4. That script becomes a regression test

The person performing the journey doesn't need to know anything about testing. They just use the app. The traces do the rest — because wrapped components emit enough semantic detail for trace-tools to generate `getByRole('slider', { name: 'Volume' })` instead of brittle CSS selectors.

Hand-written renderers can't participate in this workflow. They don't trace, and they don't forward the `aria-label` that makes the generated selectors meaningful.
      ]]>
    </Markdown>

  </VStack>
</Component>
